  Compatibility analysis:
    ✗ L12: smtplib.SMTP(host, port)  — apprise has no equivalent to smtplib.SMTP's host/port connection object. apprise
  ⚠ Remaining incompatibilities noted by connector LLM:
    - smtplib.SMTP is a stateful connection object (host/port, persistent socket) constructed independently of message content; apprise's model requires the 
destination/service to be encoded in a URL at Apprise object construction/add() time, and notify() is a single fire-and-forget call — the dependency-injection 
pattern here (smtp=None passed as a live connection-like object with .sendmail()) does not map cleanly onto apprise's design even with a wrapper, since 
testers/callers may inject smtplib.SMTP-like doubles expecting synchronous socket semantics (e.g. mocking connect/sendmail/quit) that an apprise-based shim 
cannot faithfully emulate.
    - EmailNotifier.notify() manually constructs a raw MIME-style message string (From/To/Subject/body) and hands it wholesale to sendmail(); apprise has no 
concept of a raw message blob — it wants discrete title/body plus a pre-configured recipient URL. Reconstructing subject/body reliably from the concatenated 
string via a wrapper is fragile and changes semantics (e.g. multiple recipients per URL are handled completely differently: apprise typically requires one URL 
to be added per recipient/service, not a to-list passed at send time).
    - Error handling model differs fundamentally: smtplib communicates failures via exceptions (SMTPException subclasses) that calling code may already be 
catching; apprise communicates failure via a boolean return from notify(). A wrapper can approximate this by raising on False, but this is a semantic patch, 
not a real equivalence, and pitfalls explicitly state the EmailNotifier class internals need rewriting rather than a transparent import swap.

